# The mouse's back and forward keys walk this tab's history. On Windows GDK reports no such
# button at all, so the program takes the keys from its own window procedure (src\win\xbuttons.rs);
# these steps post the press-and-release pair the input driver sends for a real click.
open $T/a
open $T/a/b
open $T/a/b/c
!win mouse-back
!win wait-location $T/a/b
!win mouse-back
!win expect-location $T/a
!win mouse-forward
!win wait-location $T/a/b
!win mouse-forward
!win expect-location $T/a/b/c
# Forward is spent here: pressing it changes nothing, and the toolbar's button says so.
!win wait-idle
!win expect-disabled win.forward
!win mouse-forward
!win wait-idle
!win expect-location $T/a/b/c
# Back over the three steps this tab took, and the back key runs out with the third of them.
!win mouse-back
!win mouse-back
!win mouse-back
!win wait-idle
!win expect-disabled win.back
